WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.233 --> 00:00:02.167 line:7%
- My name is Jordan Kepler.

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:03.235 line:7%
I'm from Santa Ynez, California.

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:04.871 line:0%
I am a full-time
graphic designer

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.172 line:7%
and a part-time bladesmith.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.942
I do a lot of camping
out of my truck.

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:11.377
Out there, having one knife
that you can chop wood,

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:14.481
make a campfire, prep
dinner and all that stuff

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:17.183
is pretty important.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.351
 My name is Jesse Overton.

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:21.321 line:0%
I'm 28, and I'm from
Medford, Oregon.

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:23.289
I was in the Marine
Corps for four years.

00:00:23.355 --> 00:00:25.391
Being in the Marine Corps,
one of the biggest things

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:28.094
that we pride ourselves
on is being able to adapt,

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:29.161
and being flexible.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:30.696
And I think that
all those things

00:00:30.796 --> 00:00:32.398
really are going to make me
prepared for this competition.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.134
GRADY POWELL: Jordan,
Jesse, congratulations.

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:36.569
It is now between the two of you
to figure out who's going home

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.571
with the title of "Forged
in Fire" Champion,

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.206
and a check for $10,000.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.707
Now, in the final
round of competition,

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:44.410
you guys are going back home
to work on an iconic weapon

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:45.178
from history.

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:46.312
And that weapon is--

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:51.584
the headhunter's ax.

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:57.523
 The headhunter ax is
both a tool and a weapon

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.593
of the native Igorot tribe,
who hail from the mountains

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.828
of the Philippines.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.797
Featuring a sharp
spike on one side that

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.598
is used to pierce
shields and armor,

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.667
it also contained a
wide, lethal ax head

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.303
on the other side designed
to swiftly decapitate

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:11.871
enemies in the heat of battle.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.640
An intimidating
weapon, tribes would

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.908
display the heads of
their victims to strike

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:17.209
fear into their enemies.

00:01:17.276 --> 00:01:19.746
The legacy of this
deadly ax lives on today,

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:21.146
and can be seen
in the video game,

00:01:21.213 --> 00:01:23.249
"State of Decay, Breakdown."

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:24.317
GRADY POWELL: So good luck.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:26.486
We will see you in three days.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:27.287
 Good luck, man.

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:33.860
JORDAN KEPLER: We're back,
day one at my home forge

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:35.261
here in Santa Ynez, California.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.296
I'm going to be making
a headhunter's ax.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.866
We're getting close to
forge welding temperature.

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.467
But this is definitely
a pivotal moment,

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:44.470
to know that my forge welder is
set, and they're really nice,

00:01:44.536 --> 00:01:47.539
and it's a homogeneous piece
of steel all the way through.

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:49.375
So this has got to
be perfect right now.

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:58.317
I'm really excited to see that
it is actually forge welded.

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:00.686
Today, the biggest hurdle is
going to be that heat treat.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.088
That's a weird shape.

00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:04.223
Let's hope everything goes well.

00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:06.626
That thing just doesn't
fit in there very well.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:09.562
This is a little nerve-racking,
making sure that it's perfectly

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:12.531
heated across the board.

00:02:12.598 --> 00:02:13.266
There we go.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.037
[bleep]

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:21.574
We got a definite
warp right here.

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.176
I need to run into
my straightening jig,

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.546
straighten it, and
let it sit and wait.

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:31.651
Oh man, it's perfectly straight.

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:32.751
Yes!

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:34.387
Only one day left.

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:36.289
We're going to try and get some
pins through here, to make sure

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:38.958
that not only we have a chemical
connection to the blade,

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.060
but we have a
mechanical connection.

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.595
We're through!

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.030
And now, I'm going
to get everything

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.431
pretty much to final finish.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:54.607
That is looking mean!

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.075
Yeah, she's sharp.

00:02:56.142 --> 00:02:57.577
[laughs]

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:01.414
JESSE OVERTON: I
am back at my home

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:02.715
forge here in Medford, Oregon.

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:04.584
I don't really have any
time to make mistakes.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:07.086
Kind of my strategy is to
draw out the spike end first.

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.589
That's going to be the hardest
and longest thing to draw out.

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:13.058
So I get done forging, and
now, it's time for the quench.

00:03:13.158 --> 00:03:15.227
And I'm feeling pretty nervous.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:16.663
I feel like my guts
are in my throat.

00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:24.837
Oh yeah.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:25.638
We did it!

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:26.639
It's hard!

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:28.440
We're sitting pretty
good for day two.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.843
I'm getting all the final
touches on the ax head.

00:03:30.943 --> 00:03:33.613
I really want to make sure that
I get this ax nice and sharp.

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:37.617
I opened up a seam where my
weld met the actual socket.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:40.152
If I weld on this
for too long, then

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.788
it's going to ruin
my heat treat.

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:44.090
I think we got it.

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:46.024
The bladesmithing gods
have looked out for me.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:47.059
The gap is gone.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.193
So relieved right now.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.261
I was doing a little
bit of research

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:50.529
on the warriors of this tribe.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:52.798
And I noticed that
there's certain tattoos

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:54.833
that they give warriors
after they behead somebody.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.903
So I'm going to incorporate
some of those designs

00:03:58.004 --> 00:03:59.138
into the handle.

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:00.172
That way, it'll give
some good traction,

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:02.141
but also look bad ass.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.110
Nice, thick coat on there.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.612
It's looking really nice.

00:04:05.712 --> 00:04:08.113
But I really want to see if
my edge geometry is there.

00:04:08.213 --> 00:04:09.582
So I decided to do a cut test.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:14.220
[laughing]

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:16.622
Oh, I feel so much better!

00:04:16.722 --> 00:04:19.591
On round two of my knife,
it did not cut very well.

00:04:19.691 --> 00:04:21.627
So to know that I'm
actually able to make

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:24.396
something that can cut, and do
it well, it feels really good.

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:26.599
I'd say it's a pass, in my book.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:28.400
GRADY POWELL: Gentlemen,
welcome back to the forge.

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.403
Both your blades look
absolutely deadly.

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:33.172
But there's really only one
way we can find out if they

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:34.507
are as deadly as they look.

00:04:34.573 --> 00:04:36.742
We've got a strength
test, a sharpness test.

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:38.278
And up first, the kill.

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:46.786
 Bladesmiths, welcome
to the kill test.

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:49.355
The Igorot headhunter's ax--

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.291
a weapon that I've
always fantasized

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:54.227
about, because it's very
close to my Filipino heritage.

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:55.828
To find out what
kind of lethal damage

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.964
your headhunter's ax will
do, I will take your weapon

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:02.501
and deliver some lethal blows
on this ballistics dummy.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:03.970
Jordan, are you ready?

00:05:04.037 --> 00:05:04.737
Because I am.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:05.338
 I think so.

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:07.640
 Let's do this.

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:09.542
JORDAN KEPLER: Right now,
I'm pretty intimidated.

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:11.910
If that spike hits a
backbone, or the skull,

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:14.647
or anything like that, that
thing could snap right off.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.853
 [shouting]

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:37.703
DOUG MARCAIDA:
All right, Jordan,

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.172
let's talk about your
headhunter's ax here.

00:05:40.238 --> 00:05:42.108
Your edges here are very sharp.

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:44.877
With every strike,
it dug in very deep

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:46.412
into this ballistics dummy.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:50.616
The spike you have here,
even digging into the skull,

00:05:50.717 --> 00:05:52.251
did not bend.

00:05:52.318 --> 00:05:53.519
It feels good in the hand.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:56.888
And more importantly,
sir, it will kill.

00:05:56.955 --> 00:05:58.224
 Thank you.

00:05:58.291 --> 00:05:59.759
 All right, Jesse,
you're up next.

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:00.292
Are you ready?

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:01.159
 I'm ready.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.061
 Let's do this.

00:06:03.128 --> 00:06:04.430
 I'm feeling nervous.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.399
I'm worried about my ax
head flying off the socket,

00:06:06.465 --> 00:06:07.900
because those bones are dense.

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:12.304
You know, going into a human
skull is not an easy task.

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:14.774
 [shouting]

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:32.792
 Ooh, he headbutted me.

00:06:37.062 --> 00:06:39.064
All right, Jesse, let's
talk about your headhunter's

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:40.299
ax over here.

00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.000
First up, the
handle construction.

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.836
So we did some research on
some Polynesian tattoos there.

00:06:44.903 --> 00:06:47.573
Looks good, and it
feels good in the hand.

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:49.675
Your edges here
penetrated very deep

00:06:49.775 --> 00:06:51.210
into this ballistics dummy.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.846
Now, your spike here, not only
did it penetrate the skull,

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:57.617
but it also cut it,
and it stayed true.

00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:01.353
Overall, sir, your
headhunter's ax will kill.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.088
 Thanks, Doug.

00:07:05.358 --> 00:07:06.325
J. NEILSON: All
right, gentlemen,

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:07.993
welcome to our strength test--

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.364
the bamboo and skull chop.

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:13.098
Now, you got two totally
different materials here.

00:07:13.198 --> 00:07:16.335
You got the springy bamboo that
likes to bounce things back,

00:07:16.435 --> 00:07:18.570
and the skulls that
like to break edges.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.140
So we're going to test both ends
and the overall construction

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:22.541
of your headhunter's axes.

00:07:22.607 --> 00:07:24.110
Jordan, you're smiling,
so you're up first.

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:25.144
How about that?

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:26.011
 Yes, sir.

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:26.979
 OK, let's do it.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.924
[shouting]

00:07:59.311 --> 00:08:00.479
Jordan, nice job.

00:08:00.545 --> 00:08:01.581
Take a breath now.

00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:03.249
 OK.

00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:05.017
J. NEILSON: Now,
everything held up nicely.

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:08.087
Everything's tight, doesn't
look like anything moved.

00:08:08.187 --> 00:08:10.589
Your edges are still sharp.

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:13.658
I mean, the fact that you
made this thing light, tough,

00:08:13.758 --> 00:08:15.460
and in Damascus in three days--

00:08:15.527 --> 00:08:16.395
that's a heck of a feat.

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:17.129
Good job.

00:08:17.196 --> 00:08:18.697
 Thank you.

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:20.999
 All right, Jesse, how
you feel after seeing that?

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:21.800
 Not good.

00:08:21.867 --> 00:08:23.068
 Not good?
It'll be fine.

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:24.036
JESSE OVERTON: OK.
- Don't worry about it.

00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:34.981
[shouting]

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:52.063
Oh!

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:53.799
[exhales deeply]

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:57.203
 Last one, man.

00:09:01.439 --> 00:09:02.641
J. NEILSON: All right, Jesse.

00:09:02.707 --> 00:09:05.945
On the plus side, it
felt great in the hand.

00:09:06.012 --> 00:09:09.048
And your edges
held up just fine.

00:09:09.114 --> 00:09:13.853
But just-- all the shockwave
from the force of those strikes

00:09:13.919 --> 00:09:16.255
split the handle almost in half.

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:18.190
So I'm definitely not going
to be able to continue

00:09:18.257 --> 00:09:19.225
testing with this weapon.

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.769
 Well, Jesse, we absolutely
hate to see that happen.

00:09:29.835 --> 00:09:31.670
Phenomenal job on
your blade itself.

00:09:31.771 --> 00:09:34.006
But unfortunately, you
had a catastrophic failure

00:09:34.073 --> 00:09:34.940
when your handle broke.

00:09:35.007 --> 00:09:36.208
And for that reason,
we can no longer

00:09:36.274 --> 00:09:37.710
continue testing your weapon.

00:09:37.777 --> 00:09:40.346
I'm going to have to ask you
to please leave the forge.

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:43.315
 I felt a little heartbroken,
seeing something that I put so

00:09:43.415 --> 00:09:44.483
much work into it to be broke.

00:09:44.583 --> 00:09:46.118
But I came onto this
competition just

00:09:46.218 --> 00:09:48.887
to prove that I can compete with
some of the best bladesmiths

00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:49.888
out there.

00:09:49.988 --> 00:09:51.424
And I know I've already
accomplished that,

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.125
so I still feel like
I'm going home a winner.

00:09:56.061 --> 00:09:58.630
 Well, Jordan, you
survived the test.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.232
Your blade came out on top.

00:10:00.298 --> 00:10:02.768
So congratulations, you are
the "Forged in Fire" Champion.

00:10:02.868 --> 00:10:04.570
And there is a
$10,000 check waiting

00:10:04.637 --> 00:10:06.005
for you outside that door.

00:10:06.072 --> 00:10:08.373
Very well done.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:09.374
 [laughs]

00:10:09.441 --> 00:10:10.675
I just won "Forged in Fire."

00:10:10.775 --> 00:10:12.744
I don't really know
what to say right now.

00:10:12.811 --> 00:10:15.313
Sasquatch is kind of my mascot,
and so I feel like that's

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.017
a Sasquatch-worthy ax.

